---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { formatPath } from '@/lib/utilities/url';
import { Terminal, Cpu, BookOpen, Layers, ArrowRight, ShieldCheck, Zap } from 'lucide-react';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'About' }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Complexity — Editorial Engineering Manifesto"
  description="Backend system design from first principles. How software systems operate, how they scale, and how they fail."
>
  <div class="max-w-reading mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <header class="mb-10 pb-6 border-b border-border-subtle">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-brand font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
        EDITORIAL MANIFESTO
      </div>
      <h1 class="font-editorial text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-text-primary tracking-tight mb-4">
        Engineering From First Principles
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-text-secondary font-technical leading-relaxed">
        Complexity is a free, open technical publication and engineering reference. We deconstruct backend architectures, protocols, operating systems, and distributed systems down to their mechanical foundations.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-complexity space-y-6">
      <h2>The Problem With Surface-Level Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        Modern software engineering documentation is often split between two extremes: theoretical academic papers that lack production context, or shallow commercial blogs and course sellers that treat complex distributed primitives as magic black boxes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Complexity exists to bridge that gap. We do not sell courses, memberships, or certifications. This is an open engineering notebook where every concept is dissected through real kernel syscalls, network socket states, packet lifecycles, and failure simulations.
      </p>

      <h2>The Seven Fundamental Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Every architecture, protocol, or design pattern covered on Complexity must answer seven uncompromising questions:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Why does it exist?</strong> What physical limitation, hardware bottleneck, or scale boundary necessitated its design?</li>
        <li><strong>What problem does it solve?</strong> Exactly what failure or inefficiency does it eliminate?</li>
        <li><strong>How does it work internally?</strong> Sockets, memory buffers, syscalls, event loops, and state transitions.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens when it receives real traffic?</strong> Connection pooling, concurrency limits, and thread contention.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens when it fails?</strong> Dropped packets, network partitions, cascading retry storms, and split-brain states.</li>
        <li><strong>What alternatives exist?</strong> Clear comparison matrices, mechanical trade-offs, and design compromises.</li>
        <li><strong>Why would an engineering team choose it?</strong> Pragmatic decision criteria for production teams.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Interactive Visual Models</h2>
      <p>
        Static text is insufficient for understanding distributed state. Throughout Complexity, articles include interactive SVG flow graphs, dynamic packet tracing simulators, and failure-injection sandboxes that let you visualize:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Client-server handshakes and TLS records moving across network layers.</li>
        <li>Buffer allocation and socket multiplexing in the Linux kernel (epoll).</li>
        <li>Cache eviction cascades, stampedes, and thundering herd mitigation in Redis.</li>
        <li>Partition leader elections and consensus log commits in distributed clusters.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Open & Independent</h2>
      <p>
        Complexity is statically generated, fast, and completely free of tracking scripts, advertisements, or paywalls. Built with Astro, React islands, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS.
      </p>

      <div class="pt-8 border-t border-border-subtle not-prose flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
        <a
          href={formatPath('/path')}
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-2.5 rounded bg-brand text-white font-mono text-xs font-semibold hover:bg-brand-hover transition-colors"
        >
          <span>Explore Structured Path</span>
          <ArrowRight className="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
        </a>
        <a
          href={formatPath('/articles')}
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-2.5 rounded bg-surface-elevated border border-border-subtle text-text-primary font-mono text-xs font-semibold hover:border-brand transition-colors"
        >
          <span>Browse All Articles</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
